[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Externalized Configuration in Spring Boot"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Boot provides a powerful mechanism for externalizing configuration, allowing you to use the same application code in different environments without recompiling. This is achieved through various sources like properties files, YAML files, and environment variables. This tutorial will guide you through using these externalized configuration options effectively."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Properties Files"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Properties files are the simplest way to externalize configuration in Spring Boot. By default, Spring Boot automatically loads `application.properties` from the following locations (in order of precedence):"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A `/config` subdirectory of the current directory."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The current directory."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A `/config` package."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The classpath root."},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also specify the location of your properties file using the `spring.config.location` property. This can be set as an environment variable or a command-line argument."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: application.properties"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"server.port=8081
spring.application.name=my-spring-boot-app
my.custom.property=Hello from properties file"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Accessing Properties in Your Code"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can access properties defined in `application.properties` using the `@Value` annotation or the `@ConfigurationProperties` annotation."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"@Value Annotation"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class MyComponent {

    @Value("${my.custom.property}")
    private String myCustomProperty;

    public String getMyCustomProperty() {
        return myCustomProperty;
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `@Value` annotation injects the value of the `my.custom.property` into the `myCustomProperty` field."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"@ConfigurationProperties Annotation"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "my.custom")
public class MyCustomProperties {

    private String property;

    public String getProperty() {
        return property;
    }

    public void setProperty(String property) {
        this.property = property;
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `@ConfigurationProperties` binds properties with the prefix `my.custom` to the fields of the `MyCustomProperties` class.  Make sure to include the `spring-boot-configuration-processor` dependency in your `pom.xml` to enable auto-completion and validation for your custom configuration properties."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-configuration-processor</artifactId>
    <optional>true</optional>
</dependency>"},{"order":21,"type":"heading","text":"YAML Files"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"YAML (YAML Ain't Markup Language) is a human-readable data serialization format. Spring Boot supports YAML files for configuration using `application.yml` or `application.yaml`. YAML files offer a more structured and readable way to define configuration properties compared to properties files."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: application.yml"},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"server:
  port: 8082
spring:
  application:
    name: my-spring-boot-app
my:
  custom:
    property: Hello from YAML file"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"The properties can be accessed in the same way as with properties files, using `@Value` or `@ConfigurationProperties`."},{"order":26,"type":"heading","text":"Environment Variables"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Environment variables are system-level variables that can be accessed by applications. Spring Boot automatically picks up environment variables and makes them available as configuration properties. Environment variables take precedence over properties defined in `application.properties` or `application.yml`."},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, if you set an environment variable `MY_CUSTOM_PROPERTY=HelloFromEnv`, you can access it in your code using `@Value("${MY_CUSTOM_PROPERTY}")`."},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Boot automatically converts environment variable names to property names. For example, an environment variable named `MY_CUSTOM_PROPERTY` is mapped to the property `my.custom.property` (case-insensitive)."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Environment Variables"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"The method for setting environment variables depends on your operating system."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Linux/macOS: `export MY_CUSTOM_PROPERTY=HelloFromEnv`"},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Windows: `set MY_CUSTOM_PROPERTY=HelloFromEnv`"},{"order":34,"type":"heading","text":"Profiles"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Profiles allow you to define environment-specific configurations. You can create separate properties or YAML files for different environments (e.g., development, testing, production)."},{"order":36,"type":"subheading","text":"Profile-Specific Configuration Files"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a profile-specific configuration file, name it `application-{profile}.properties` or `application-{profile}.yml`, where `{profile}` is the name of the profile (e.g., `application-dev.properties`, `application-prod.yml`)."},{"order":38,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: application-dev.properties"},{"order":39,"type":"code","text":"server.port=9000
my.custom.property=Hello from dev profile"},{"order":40,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: application-prod.yml"},{"order":41,"type":"code","text":"server:
  port: 80
my:
  custom:
    property: Hello from prod profile"},{"order":42,"type":"subheading","text":"Activating Profiles"},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can activate a profile using the `spring.profiles.active` property. This can be set as an environment variable, a command-line argument, or in the `application.properties` or `application.yml` file."},{"order":44,"type":"code","text":"# application.properties
spring.profiles.active=dev"},{"order":45,"type":"paragraph","text":"Alternatively, you can set it as an environment variable:"},{"order":46,"type":"code","text":"SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=dev"},{"order":47,"type":"paragraph","text":"Or as a command-line argument:"},{"order":48,"type":"code","text":"java -jar my-spring-boot-app.jar --spring.profiles.active=dev"},{"order":49,"type":"heading","text":"Command-Line Arguments"},{"order":50,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also pass configuration properties as command-line arguments when running your Spring Boot application. Command-line arguments take precedence over properties defined in properties files, YAML files, and environment variables."},{"order":51,"type":"subheading","text":"Example"},{"order":52,"type":"code","text":"java -jar my-spring-boot-app.jar --server.port=8083 --my.custom.property="Hello from command line""},{"order":53,"type":"heading","text":"Precedence of Configuration Sources"},{"order":54,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Boot loads configuration properties from various sources in a specific order of precedence. The following list shows the order from highest precedence to lowest:"},{"order":55,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Command-line arguments"},{"order":56,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Environment variables"},{"order":57,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Profile-specific `application-{profile}.properties` or `application-{profile}.yml`"},{"order":58,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`application.properties` or `application.yml`"},{"order":59,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default properties (defined in your code or libraries)"},{"order":60,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":61,"type":"paragraph","text":"Externalized configuration is a crucial aspect of building robust and maintainable Spring Boot applications. By leveraging properties files, YAML files, environment variables, and profiles, you can easily adapt your application to different environments and configurations without modifying the code. Understanding the precedence of configuration sources ensures that your application behaves as expected in various deployment scenarios."}]
